From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:57:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/color: Compress gamut toward a declared display
 target gamut

The output signal stays BT.2020/PQ, so a color outside the panel's own
gamut was left to the panel firmware, whose handling (clipping or not)
is unknown. Add gamut compression so that the decision has one known
owner: the surface to blending transform, which already maps highlights
toward the display's target.

The op, gamut_compress, is the ACES 1.3 Reference Gamut Compression
distance compression, in linear RGB of the target gamut. The
achromatic value is ach = max (R, G, B); each channel's distance from
it is d = (ach - c) / ach, 1 on the gamut boundary and above 1 outside
it. Above the threshold t = 0.8 the distance is compressed with the
published curve of power p = 1.2,

  d' = t + s x / (1 + x^p)^(1/p), x = (d - t) / s,

evaluated as t + s (1 + x^-p)^(-1/p), the same curve without overflow
for large x, and the channel becomes ach - d' ach. Samples whose
achromatic value is not positive or not finite, channels whose distance
is not above the threshold (NaN included) and channels without a limit
are not rewritten at all, so such values come out of the op bit for
bit, and finite channels never become NaN. The scale s of each channel
follows from its limit L, the distance mapped onto the boundary
(d' = 1 at d = L): s = (L - t) / (((1 - t) / (L - t))^(-p) - 1)^(1/p),
computed in double precision on the CPU when the op is built; a channel
with L <= 1 is left untouched. The op has a CPU and a GLSL
implementation with the same maths; the shader only evaluates.

The transform adds it after highlight mapping, in linear light: linear
BT.2020 is converted to linear RGB of the target primaries with
absolute colorimetry, without chromatic adaptation (BT.2020 RGB to XYZ
to target RGB, by the new clutter_colorimetry_get_absolute_bt2020_matrices()),
compressed, and converted back before the conversion to the blending
state.

Whether to compress, and with which limits, is decided by one function
that both clutter_color_transform_requires_gamut_compression() and the
op builder call, so the two cannot disagree. It returns early, without
any matrix, whenever the content is found within the target; otherwise
it computes the limits, and compresses only if some channel's limit
exceeds 1, the only case in which the op does anything.

 - Only toward a display gamut target: a blending state whose target
   volume has primaries and which carries a display reference (only
   blending states derived from a display's target volume do; a
   client's mastering primaries make no target).
 - Source colors are followed to linear BT.2020 exactly as the renderer
   converts pixels: the encoding primaries are the rows of the
   encoding's adapted conversion to linear BT.2020, Bradford adaptation
   of its white point to D65 included, and mastering display primaries
   are their XYZ with that same adaptation. For a source whose white is
   not D65 the unadapted chromaticities would be wrong: DCI-P3 with the
   DCI white has P3's chromaticities, yet its red lands outside a
   P3-D65 panel. Containment is decided on the adapted colors'
   chromaticities, and the limits on the adapted colors.
 - Only when the content can actually exceed the target: a bounded
   encoding's primaries must not all lie within the target triangle,
   and, when mastering display primaries are declared, neither must
   they. Content whose encoding fits the target, such as sRGB on a wide
   gamut panel, is never compressed, whatever its mastering metadata
   says. An extended-range linear encoding (ext_linear, scRGB-style;
   the notion nativehdr6 uses for the peak) bounds no gamut: its
   mastering primaries are the source gamut, and without them the
   gamut's extent is unknown and nothing is compressed, as nothing is
   highlight mapped without a known peak. ICC sources are not
   compressed.
 - The limits come from the mastering primaries when declared,
   otherwise from the encoding primaries, and are exact over the source
   gamut boundary. The distance is scale invariant, so a side of the
   source gamut is the set of positive combinations of its two
   primaries in target RGB; wherever its largest channel stays the
   same, each channel's distance is a linear-fractional function along
   the side, so its maximum lies at a primary or where two target
   channels are equal. Those points, up to five per side, are
   evaluated; the interior adds nothing. So no point of the source
   gamut boundary, primaries and sides, ends beyond distance 1, and
   each channel's maximising boundary point lands at 1. Points within
   the target, by its tolerances, are ignored. If one of the evaluated
   points has no positive achromatic value (the achromatic value is
   convex and piecewise linear along a side, so its minimum is among
   them) or is not finite, the boundary passes through directions the
   compression cannot map, and nothing is compressed: never NaN.

Containment, clutter_primaries_contain_chromaticity(), is defined once.
Which side of a triangle side a chromaticity lies on is decided by the
sign of a cross product of the xy coordinates in double precision, and
each primary may be off by a tolerance: a point outside a side counts
as inside if it lies no further from it than the larger tolerance of
the side's two primaries (a point of a true side is a convex
combination of its true primaries), and a point beyond both sides at a
primary must moreover lie within that primary's tolerance of it, which
clamps the band overlap that would otherwise reach
tolerance / sin (angle / 2) beyond an acute primary. With tolerances of
0 the test is exact. ClutterColorTargetVolume gains, at its end,
primaries_tolerance[3], the resolution of each primary's declaration as
an xy distance (0 for exact declarations such as a client's mastering
primaries), which takes part in equality. The adapted chromaticities
carry single precision rounding, so the decision adds a margin of the
protocol's chromaticity unit, 1e-6, to every tolerance.

clutter_colorimetry_get_linear_bt2020_matrices(), the shared definition
client and EDID primaries are accepted with, keeps the nativehdr6 rule,
the adapted pair only. The absolute pair is required, by
clutter_colorimetry_get_absolute_bt2020_matrices(), only of primaries
used as a gamut target: clutter_color_state_params_new_with_target_volume()
refuses, as a programming error, target primaries that fail the shared
definition, or whose tolerances are not finite and non-negative, and,
in a copy of a display target, primaries that fail the absolute check;
deriving a display target from a state whose target primaries fail it
drops them, loudly. A display gamut target therefore always has the
matrices the decision needs. clutter_color_state_get_blending() derives
a display target from a target volume with primaries as it does from
one with a target luminance, so a blending state carries declared target
primaries, with their tolerances, next to the target peak, with the
display reference.

The single "mapping required" predicate is now
clutter_color_transform_requires_mapping(): highlight mapping or gamut
compression. The transform builds a conversion between equal encodings
exactly when it holds, and libmutter will refuse direct scanout and the
hardware cursor plane with it.
clutter_color_transform_requires_highlight_mapping() keeps its meaning
and its export, as one part of it. Target primaries and their
tolerances take part in clutter_color_state_identical(), so the exact
transform cache tells targets with different primaries apart. The two
new predicates (the gamut one for tests) and
clutter_colorimetry_get_absolute_bt2020_matrices() (for the colour
device) are exported and listed in the symbols file at
51.0-1ubuntu3+nativehdr7~.

