+ *
+ *  - for every side the point lies outside of, its distance from that
+ *    side's line is at most the larger tolerance of the side's two
+ *    primaries: a point of a true side is a convex combination of the two
+ *    true primaries, so it lies no further outside the declared side than
+ *    the larger of their displacements;
+ *  - a point outside both sides that meet at a primary, in that primary's
+ *    outer wedge, is moreover within the primary's tolerance of it. Without
+ *    this, the two side bands alone would accept points up to
+ *    tolerance / sin (angle / 2) from an acute primary, more than any
+ *    displacement of it could explain.
